ASN.1 type Class <code>UTCTime</code> <code>Primitive\UTCTime</code> <code>GeneralizedTime</code> <code>Primitive\GeneralizedTime</code> <p>Both extend <code>BaseTime</code>: <code>create(DateTimeImmutable)</code> in, <code>dateTime(): DateTimeImmutable</code> out, and <code>fromString()</code> for the ASN.1 spelling.</p> <pre><code>use SpomkyLabs\Pki\ASN1\Type\Primitive\GeneralizedTime;

$time = GeneralizedTime::create(new DateTimeImmutable('2030-01-01 12:00:00', new DateTimeZone('UTC')));
echo $time-&gt;string();     // 20300101120000Z
</code></pre> <p><code>UTCTime</code> writes a two-digit year, which is why RFC 5280 asks for it only before 2050 and <code>GeneralizedTime</code> after. <code>UTCTime::CENTURY_PIVOT</code> is where the library splits the two centuries when it reads one. <code>Time</code> in the X.509 layer picks the right one for you.</p>

ASN.1 type Class <code>SEQUENCE</code>, <code>SEQUENCE OF</code> <code>Constructed\Sequence</code> <code>SET</code>, <code>SET OF</code> <code>Constructed\Set</code> a string in constructed form <code>Constructed\ConstructedString</code> <pre><code>use SpomkyLabs\Pki\ASN1\Type\Constructed\Set;
use SpomkyLabs\Pki\ASN1\Type\Primitive\Integer;

$set = Set::create(Integer::create(3), Integer::create(1));
$set = $set-&gt;sortedSetOf();   // DER requires the members of a SET OF to be sorted
</code></pre> <p><code>ConstructedString</code> is a string encoded as a sequence of fragments, which BER allows and DER does not. The library reads one and gives you <code>strings()</code>, the fragments, or <code>string()</code>, the whole value.</p>

self-signed.php<pre><code>&lt;?php

declare(strict_types=1);

require 'vendor/autoload.php';

use SpomkyLabs\Pki\CryptoEncoding\PEM;
use SpomkyLabs\Pki\CryptoTypes\AlgorithmIdentifier\Signature\SHA256WithRSAEncryptionAlgorithmIdentifier;
use SpomkyLabs\Pki\CryptoTypes\Asymmetric\PrivateKeyInfo;
use SpomkyLabs\Pki\X501\ASN1\Name;
use SpomkyLabs\Pki\X509\Certificate\Extension\BasicConstraintsExtension;
use SpomkyLabs\Pki\X509\Certificate\Extension\ExtendedKeyUsageExtension;
use SpomkyLabs\Pki\X509\Certificate\Extension\KeyUsageExtension;
use SpomkyLabs\Pki\X509\Certificate\Extension\SubjectAlternativeNameExtension;
use SpomkyLabs\Pki\X509\Certificate\Extension\SubjectKeyIdentifierExtension;
use SpomkyLabs\Pki\X509\Certificate\TBSCertificate;
use SpomkyLabs\Pki\X509\Certificate\Validity;
use SpomkyLabs\Pki\X509\GeneralName\DNSName;
use SpomkyLabs\Pki\X509\GeneralName\GeneralNames;

$privateKeyInfo = PrivateKeyInfo::fromPEM(PEM::fromFile('key.pem'));
$publicKeyInfo = $privateKeyInfo-&gt;publicKeyInfo();

$name = Name::fromString('cn=example.com, o=ACME Ltd, c=FR');

// subject, key, issuer, validity. Self-signed: the subject is its own issuer.
$tbsCertificate = TBSCertificate::create(
    $name,
    $publicKeyInfo,
    $name,
    Validity::fromStrings('now', 'now + 1 year')
);

$tbsCertificate = $tbsCertificate-&gt;withAdditionalExtensions(
    BasicConstraintsExtension::create(true, false),
    KeyUsageExtension::create(
        true,
        KeyUsageExtension::DIGITAL_SIGNATURE | KeyUsageExtension::KEY_ENCIPHERMENT
    ),
    ExtendedKeyUsageExtension::create(false, ExtendedKeyUsageExtension::OID_SERVER_AUTH),
    SubjectAlternativeNameExtension::create(false, GeneralNames::create(
        DNSName::create('example.com'),
        DNSName::create('www.example.com')
    )),
    SubjectKeyIdentifierExtension::create(false, $publicKeyInfo-&gt;keyIdentifier())
);

// sign() draws a 20-octet random serial number, since none was set.
$certificate = $tbsCertificate-&gt;sign(
    SHA256WithRSAEncryptionAlgorithmIdentifier::create(),
    $privateKeyInfo
);

file_put_contents('cert.pem', $certificate-&gt;toPEM()-&gt;string());

echo "written: cert.pem\n";
</code></pre> <pre><code>php self-signed.php
</code></pre>

